      </div>
    </div>

    <section id="projects" aria-labelledby="projects-h">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="projects-h">Projects</h2>
        <p class="intro">Things I've built and published. The code is public.</p>

        <div class="projects">
          <article class="card" aria-labelledby="p-anchor">
            <div class="media">
              <video
                class="autoplay"
                muted
                loop
                playsinline
                controls
                preload="none"
                width="1280"
                height="720"
                poster={m('tour.jpg')}
                aria-label="Claude Anchor menu showing several Claude accounts, folder rules and the 5-hour and weekly limit rings."
              >
                <source src={m('tour-silent.webm')} type="video/webm" />
                <source src={m('tour-silent.mp4')} type="video/mp4" />
              </video>
            </div>
            <p class="media-note">
              A 26-second tour of Claude Anchor. <a href="https://github.com/SumithSB/claude-anchor/blob/main/docs/tour.mp4">Watch with sound</a>
            </p>
            <div>
              <h3 id="p-anchor">Claude Anchor</h3>
              <p class="summary">Pin a folder to a Claude account. Claude Code then uses the right account in your terminal, VS Code and Cursor.</p>
            </div>
            <p class="outcome">3 platforms · 5 shells and editors · 2 rate-limit windows tracked</p>
            <ul class="tags" aria-label="Stack">
              <li>Swift</li><li>SwiftUI</li><li>Rust</li><li>Tauri</li><li>Shell scripting</li><li>Anthropic OAuth usage API</li><li>Homebrew</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="links">
              <a href="https://github.com/SumithSB/claude-anchor">Claude Anchor on GitHub</a>
              <a href="https://x.com/SumithSBhandari/status/2110735490886365441">Launch post on X</a>
            </div>
            <details>
              <summary>Read the case study</summary>
              <div class="case">
                <div>
                  <h4>The problem</h4>
                  <p>I use different Claude accounts for different kinds of work, like work and personal. Claude Code only knows one login at a time. Switching meant logging out and back in, and it was easy to end up using the wrong account in the wrong project.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4>What I built</h4>
                  <p>A desktop app that keeps the right Claude account for each kind of work. You pin a folder to an account once. From then on, opening that folder in a terminal, VS Code or Cursor uses that account, with no manual switching. It runs as a menu bar app on macOS and a tray app on Windows and Linux, with an <code>anchor</code> command line tool.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4>How it works</h4>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Each extra account gets its own Claude Code config folder. Shared settings, skills and plugins are linked in, so every account feels the same.</li>
                    <li>A small routes file maps folders to accounts. The longest matching folder wins, and there is a default for everything else.</li>
                    <li>A shell hook picks the account when you run <code>claude</code>. VS Code and Cursor are routed through Claude Code's process wrapper setting.</li>
                    <li>Conversation history is shared per folder, so you can continue a conversation on another account with its history.</li>
                    <li>Because it already knows all your accounts, it also shows each one's 5-hour and weekly limits, and can move new sessions to an account with headroom so you don't stall at the limit.</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4>How I checked it</h4>
                  <ul>
                    <li>No telemetry. The only network traffic is to Anthropic's usage endpoint.</li>
                    <li>Login tokens are read only to check limits. They are never stored, logged or sent anywhere else.</li>
                    <li>Installers verify a SHA-256 checksum before installing. Your existing Claude Code folder is never changed, and editor settings are backed up before they are edited.</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4>Outcome</h4>
                  <ul>
                    <li>Runs on 3 platforms: macOS, Windows and Linux.</li>
                    <li>Works in 5 shells and editors: zsh, bash, PowerShell, VS Code and Cursor.</li>
                    <li>Tracks 2 rate-limit windows per account: 5-hour and weekly.</li>
                    <li>Open source under MIT. Install with Homebrew or a checksum-verified script.</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <h4>Limits and what's next</h4>
                  <p>Unofficial and not affiliated with Anthropic. It relies on Claude Code behaviour that isn't documented and could change. A running session can't change account; new sessions follow the rules. Next on the roadmap: more shells (fish, cmd.exe) and other coding agent CLIs.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="gallery">
                  <figure>
                    <picture>
                      <source srcset={m('popover-light.webp')} type="image/webp" />
                      <img src={m('popover-light.png')} width="680" height="2192" loading="lazy" alt="Claude Anchor menu." />
                    </picture>
